{"name":"Climate Change","hex":"#e5e1cd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"climate change","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Natural large-scale and long-term change in the Earth's climatic system, as brought about by ice ages.","Rapid, large-scale, and long-term change in the Earth's climatic system produced by global warming; anthropogenic climate change."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/climate_change"}],"entries":[{"title":"Climate change","blocks":[{"text":"Present-day climate change includes both global warming—the ongoing increase in global average temperature—and its wider effects on Earth's climate system. In a broader sense, climate change also includes previous long-term changes to Earth's climate. The modern-day rise in global temperatures is driven by human activities, especially fossil fuel burning since the Industrial Revolution. Fossil fuel use, deforestation, and some agricultural and industrial practices release greenhouse gases. These gases absorb some of the heat that the Earth radiates after it warms from sunlight, warming the lower atmosphere. Earth's atmosphere now has roughly 50% more carbon dioxide, the main gas driving global warming, than it did at the end of the pre-industrial era, reaching levels not seen for millions of years."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Change_in_Average_Temperature_With_Fahrenheit.svg/330px-Change_in_Average_Temperature_With_Fahrenheit.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_change"},{"title":"Climate","blocks":[{"text":"Climate is the long-term weather pattern in a region, typically averaged over 30 years. More rigorously, it is the mean and variability of meteorological variables over a time spanning from months to millions of years. Some of the meteorological variables that are commonly measured are temperature, humidity, atmospheric pressure, wind, and precipitation. In a broader sense, climate is the state of the components of the climate system, including the atmosphere, hydrosphere, cryosphere, lithosphere and biosphere and the interactions between them. The climate of a location is affected by its latitude, longitude, terrain, altitude, land use and nearby water bodies and their currents."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/ISS-48_Towering_cumulonimbus_and_other_clouds_over_the_Earth_%282%29.jpg/330px-ISS-48_Towering_cumulonimbus_and_other_clouds_over_the_Earth_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Bobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg/500px-Bobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Bobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg/1280px-Bobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Bobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg/500px-Bobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Bleached_colony_of_Acropora_coral.jpg/500px-Bleached_colony_of_Acropora_coral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Bleached_colony_of_Acropora_coral.jpg/1280px-Bleached_colony_of_Acropora_coral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Bleached_colony_of_Acropora_coral.jpg/500px-Bleached_colony_of_Acropora_coral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABleached_colony_of_Acropora_coral.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/California_Drought_Dry_Lakebed_2009.jpg/500px-California_Drought_Dry_Lakebed_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/California_Drought_Dry_Lakebed_2009.jpg/500px-California_Drought_Dry_Lakebed_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACalifornia_Drought_Dry_Lakebed_2009.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/NORTH_POLE_Ice_%2819626661335%29.jpg/500px-NORTH_POLE_Ice_%2819626661335%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/NORTH_POLE_Ice_%2819626661335%29.jpg/1280px-NORTH_POLE_Ice_%2819626661335%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/NORTH_POLE_Ice_%2819626661335%29.jpg/500px-NORTH_POLE_Ice_%2819626661335%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sea ice reflects 50% to 70% of incoming sunlight, while the ocean, being darker, reflects only 6%. As an area of sea ice melts and exposes more ocean, more heat is absorbed by the ocean, raising temperatures that melt still more ice. This is a positive feedback process.[158]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANORTH_POLE_Ice_(19626661335).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Tipping_points_2022_list.jpeg/500px-Tipping_points_2022_list.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Tipping_points_2022_list.jpeg/1280px-Tipping_points_2022_list.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Tipping_points_2022_list.jpeg/500px-Tipping_points_2022_list.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Different levels of global warming may cause different parts of Earth's climate system to reach tipping points that cause transitions to different states.[210][211]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATipping_points_2022_list.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Bleachedcoral.jpg/250px-Bleachedcoral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Bleachedcoral.jpg/500px-Bleachedcoral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Bleachedcoral.jpg/250px-Bleachedcoral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABleachedcoral.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Orroral_Valley_Fire_viewed_from_Tuggeranong_January_2020.jpg/250px-Orroral_Valley_Fire_viewed_from_Tuggeranong_January_2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Orroral_Valley_Fire_viewed_from_Tuggeranong_January_2020.jpg/500px-Orroral_Valley_Fire_viewed_from_Tuggeranong_January_2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Orroral_Valley_Fire_viewed_from_Tuggeranong_January_20